00:00:36.421 --> 00:00:38.990
eco friendly for your own children.

00:00:38.990 --> 00:00:43.505
So can you tell us about how these
pain coins led you to developing

00:00:43.505 --> 00:00:45.103
a business solution?

00:00:45.103 --> 00:00:47.195
&gt;&gt; Yeah, gladly.

00:00:47.195 --> 00:00:52.018
I think it really was for me,

00:00:55.425 --> 00:01:02.325
Filling a need and
finding a real opportunity to show up,

00:01:02.325 --> 00:01:08.378
not just for myself, but
for many people in a way.

00:01:08.378 --> 00:01:13.081
I think I was seeing that
companies that make products

00:01:13.081 --> 00:01:17.679
that really interact with
you every single day, and

00:01:17.679 --> 00:01:23.830
could have an effect on your health and
wellbeing over time.

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:27.881
They just weren't really
considering I think,

00:01:27.881 --> 00:01:33.220
holistically your health and
wellness as the top priority.

00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:37.875
And for me, I just felt like our
health and wellness, like when you go

00:01:37.875 --> 00:01:42.718
through your life and you are thinking
of quality of life and thriving.

00:01:42.718 --> 00:01:47.074
And when your health is compromised,
and your health and

00:01:47.074 --> 00:01:50.521
wellness is compromised in any way,
shape or

00:01:50.521 --> 00:01:56.329
form [COUGH] it's really difficult to
then live a life where you can thrive and

00:01:56.329 --> 00:02:00.170
be happy [COUGH] and
live life to the fullest.

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.843
So when I was a child,
and I was very sick and

00:02:03.843 --> 00:02:08.552
I was constantly reminded
that I wasn't like others.

00:02:08.552 --> 00:02:14.355
And I was isolated,
I had severe asthma and allergies.

00:02:14.355 --> 00:02:17.894
And every time I had
an allergy to something,

00:02:17.894 --> 00:02:23.870
it actually triggered my asthma, and
I was put in the hospital on steroids.

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:29.769
I was in the hospital, I would say
at least three or four times a year,

00:02:29.769 --> 00:02:33.366
for a week to two weeks
doing steroid treatments and

00:02:33.366 --> 00:02:38.159
breathing treatments and
dealing with pneumonia and bronchitis.

00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:43.465
And every cold turned into pneumonia,
so it was just like a whole situation.

00:02:43.465 --> 00:02:48.390
[COUGH] But I wanted to play baseball and
soccer and be a normal kid.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:51.270
And so I would go on the soccer
field with a breathing machine.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.890
And I would have that next to me.

00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.563
I also had kidney issues and cysts and

00:02:58.563 --> 00:03:04.594
many different other
surgeries on top of that,

00:03:04.594 --> 00:03:08.830
and all of this before I was 11.

00:03:08.830 --> 00:03:13.354
So I just dealt with
a lot of health issues.

00:03:13.354 --> 00:03:16.702
And when I was pregnant
with my first daughter,

00:03:16.702 --> 00:03:20.385
about 13 years ago,
I had an allergic reaction and

00:03:20.385 --> 00:03:25.680
I had pretty much stayed away from
the things that were causing reactions.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.067
And I started to really create
sort of this subconsciously,

00:03:30.067 --> 00:03:33.851
the safe environment where
I wasn't exposed to a lot

00:03:33.851 --> 00:03:38.800
of the things that would
trigger my allergies or asthma.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.067
And then when I was pregnant with Honor,
I had an allergic reaction

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:50.690
to a detergent that was marketed to
parents to use on their baby's clothing.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:55.340
And my mom said she used it on me and
I didn't know very many moms.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.917
And when I had this allergic reaction,
I got like a flood of

00:03:58.917 --> 00:04:03.014
the memories of me as a kid, and
having all of these health issues.

00:04:03.014 --> 00:04:07.245
And I was like, my god,
what if my baby is like me?

00:04:07.245 --> 00:04:14.156
What if she has these sort of issues, and
it really kind of shaped who I am and

00:04:14.156 --> 00:04:19.536
I think it builds character to
go through difficulties but

00:04:19.536 --> 00:04:23.840
it could be avoided, let's avoid them.

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.877
I also was dealing with, sorry there's
a plane, they like planes over here.

00:04:29.877 --> 00:04:35.094
[LAUGH]
My

00:04:35.094 --> 00:04:40.598
mother got cancer when she was,
I believe she was 23,

00:04:40.598 --> 00:04:45.648
22 or 23, and
had to have a full hysterectomy.

00:04:45.648 --> 00:04:48.200
And meaning she couldn't
have children any more.

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:53.062
And her cancer was environmentally caused
because it was nothing genetically that

00:04:53.062 --> 00:04:55.000
was linked to that.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.862
So when I sort of looked, I like took
sort of a reflection on my childhood,

00:05:00.862 --> 00:05:03.650
my mom's health and wellness.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.484
Now I'm bringing this little
person into the world and

00:05:06.484 --> 00:05:09.540
thinking about quality of
life in a different way.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:15.356
I was told I wasn't allowed to eat deli
meats, and I couldn't use my retinol on

00:05:15.356 --> 00:05:21.190
my face because it could possibly affect
the baby and her health and wellness.

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:25.300
So I started looking at ingredients and
thinking about my health differently and

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:26.215
hers obviously.

00:05:26.215 --> 00:05:29.508
And I learned about lots of untested, and

00:05:29.508 --> 00:05:36.150
potentially harmful chemicals that we
use and are exposed to on the daily.

00:05:36.150 --> 00:05:42.375
And a lot of it is in beauty products,
hair, skin products,

00:05:42.375 --> 00:05:47.763
makeup products, fragrances,
home detergents.

00:05:47.763 --> 00:05:51.843
And so then I was like, my goodness every
every single day we're being exposed

00:05:51.843 --> 00:05:55.863
to all of these potentially harmful
chemicals, and we don't even know it, and

00:05:55.863 --> 00:05:58.514
we don't even know how
it's affecting our health.

00:05:58.514 --> 00:06:02.107
So then I was looking at,
I looked at data and

00:06:02.107 --> 00:06:08.250
was trying to understand the research
around the rise of illness.

00:06:08.250 --> 00:06:13.738
And there was a pretty parallel
path in the industrialization

00:06:13.738 --> 00:06:21.810
of these chemicals and then being put into
consumer products and beauty products.

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:27.291
And that rise really
paralleled the rise of many

00:06:27.291 --> 00:06:32.644
illnesses that people
are dealing with from.

00:06:32.644 --> 00:06:35.395
Because they're hormone disruptors,
and they're endocrine disruptors.

00:06:35.395 --> 00:06:41.362
[COUGH] And they're linked to
things like various cancers,

00:06:41.362 --> 00:06:48.771
learning disabilities, and also
the way that it affects your hormones.

00:06:48.771 --> 00:06:55.826
It's basically giving your body steroids.

00:06:55.826 --> 00:06:59.464
And it really screws up your hormones,
men and women,

00:06:59.464 --> 00:07:02.244
the exposure to a lot of these chemicals.

00:07:02.244 --> 00:07:05.888
So, I was like how do we
not know about this, and

00:07:05.888 --> 00:07:09.078
how is this even allowed in this country,

00:07:09.078 --> 00:07:14.656
shouldn't there be a governing sort
of practice or rules or standards?

00:07:14.656 --> 00:07:20.546
And in the UK they actually make companies
test chemicals for safetybBefore they

00:07:20.546 --> 00:07:26.451
are even allowed to be brought into the
market place in personal care and beauty.

00:07:26.451 --> 00:07:31.442
And in this country, the way that the
system is set up is it has to be proven to

00:07:31.442 --> 00:07:36.920
kill enough people before they'll
even look into it being dangerous.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.726
So I was like, what?

00:07:38.726 --> 00:07:42.975
So basically we're just being tested on?

00:07:42.975 --> 00:07:45.928
And I was like that is so messed up.

00:07:45.928 --> 00:07:51.757
And then people who live in
a sort of different circumstances

00:07:51.757 --> 00:07:57.703
where they can't necessarily
control their environment and

00:07:57.703 --> 00:08:03.464
their shopping Habits because
they're just trying to survive and

00:08:03.464 --> 00:08:06.961
they're living paycheck to
paycheck like I grew up.

00:08:06.961 --> 00:08:13.241
And a lot of black and brown families
as well are disproportionately

00:08:13.241 --> 00:08:18.210
affected by the exposure to
a lot of these chemicals.

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:22.999
And so I was just like wow, and so I
legislated for chemical reform to try and

00:08:22.999 --> 00:08:27.950
at least get our standards in this
country to be more like Europe.

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:32.377
And a lot sorry,
not legislated, I lobbied for

00:08:32.377 --> 00:08:37.468
the legislation for
chemical reform, excuse me, and

00:08:37.468 --> 00:08:42.449
I did it twice and
basically as you know during COVID,

00:08:42.449 --> 00:08:46.360
our health has been politicized.

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:50.972
And, it's basically whatever
opinion someone wants to

00:08:50.972 --> 00:08:55.780
decide is truth becomes the truth,
versus facts.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:09:00.760
And so, I was like, wow, we this
shouldn't be about a political party.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:04.321
This is just common sense and
human health,

00:09:04.321 --> 00:09:08.530
anyway I'm frustrated with the system.

00:09:08.530 --> 00:09:13.366
I was like,
well how can I create a solution for

00:09:13.366 --> 00:09:17.210
people that really has the ethics and

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:21.307
values around it human health safety?

00:09:21.307 --> 00:09:25.984
Also, thinking about the planet,
[LAUGH] and sustainability.

00:09:25.984 --> 00:09:28.811
[LAUGH] And
then obviously I think the last and

00:09:28.811 --> 00:09:33.474
the most important piece of the puzzle
is really showing up for people and

00:09:33.474 --> 00:09:37.464
meeting their needs no matter
where they live in the country.

00:09:37.464 --> 00:09:43.085
It shouldn't be like I afford
the most expensive things and

00:09:43.085 --> 00:09:48.602
so I can shop at a whole foods or
whatever type of grocery store,

00:09:48.602 --> 00:09:52.410
but everyone else is sort of left out.

00:09:52.410 --> 00:09:54.660
And I was like, that just isn't fair.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:58.042
And also I felt like there weren't a lot
of companies that stood for health and

00:09:58.042 --> 00:09:58.570
wellness.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:02.112
That also like spoke my language.

00:10:02.112 --> 00:10:07.197
I don't know I just feel like
I love beauty in design and

00:10:07.197 --> 00:10:10.635
I love beautiful things in the home.

00:10:10.635 --> 00:10:16.431
And I just felt like there was a way to
kind of like show up in a beautiful way,

00:10:16.431 --> 00:10:22.040
but still have the ethos and the values
and ethics that I had as a person.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:25.720
So that's really where the business
came from, and the idea, and

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.850
that's how it started.

00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:30.269
&gt;&gt; Well,
I love the intergenerational aspect of it,

00:10:30.269 --> 00:10:34.154
of watching your mom go through something,
experiencing something yourself and

00:10:34.154 --> 00:10:36.650
then trying to create a solution for
your children.

00:10:36.650 --> 00:10:40.440
That's obviously a really
wonderful brand story.

00:10:40.440 --> 00:10:45.530
But obviously not everyone was sold
in the beginning, so I'm curious.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:50.789
How did your business pitch evolve as you
experienced some initial naysayers or

00:10:50.789 --> 00:10:52.220
some knows?

00:10:52.220 --> 00:10:57.870
&gt;&gt; Yeah, and I think you have to know
your audience, know their capacity.

00:10:57.870 --> 00:11:02.786
And originally I was pitching this
idea to start this business with

00:11:02.786 --> 00:11:05.320
people in entertainment.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:11.105
Not knowing that the very business model
in entertainment is incoming calls and

00:11:11.105 --> 00:11:13.410
licensing deals, right?

00:11:13.410 --> 00:11:19.240
And it isn't starting from the ground
up and investing in a concept.

00:11:19.240 --> 00:11:23.722
Now I think Hollywood has gotten
a little bit more savvy and

00:11:23.722 --> 00:11:28.389
I would say the success of my
company has moved the business to

00:11:28.389 --> 00:11:34.070
be a little bit more open,
two different ways of operating.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:41.249
I also think that because
the shift in access to content and

00:11:41.249 --> 00:11:45.791
the Internet and habits changing and

00:11:45.791 --> 00:11:52.531
consuming has changed so
dramatically over the last,

00:11:52.531 --> 00:11:58.060
I mean 20 years, but really ten years.

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:03.831
So the business Hollywood has had to also
try and figure out where they fit in that.

00:12:03.831 --> 00:12:08.815
And I think they're still challenged
because they're holding on

00:12:08.815 --> 00:12:13.977
to a lot of the old sort of systems,
but yeah, so I guess I was pitching

00:12:13.977 --> 00:12:18.710
this idea to the wrong audience,
that's first and foremost.

00:12:18.710 --> 00:12:24.620
And then I started pitching it around to
different people outside of my scope.

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:29.828
And I would say,
while as an entrepreneur, you're so

00:12:29.828 --> 00:12:34.254
excited and
you see you're end goal, right?

00:12:34.254 --> 00:12:39.566
And you're moving towards that end goal,
you have to make it palatable for

00:12:39.566 --> 00:12:42.702
what can happen here, and now, and today.

00:12:42.702 --> 00:12:48.247
So it's like well you want to sell
the dream of this is going to be a global

00:12:48.247 --> 00:12:54.101
brand that stands for the health and
well being of people in the planet.

00:12:54.101 --> 00:12:59.615
And we're going to make not only build
community and educate people on how they

00:12:59.615 --> 00:13:05.144
can live a better life, but we're also
going to create the solution for them.

00:13:05.144 --> 00:13:09.200
And it's going to to be
in various categories,

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:14.432
around home detergents,
around bath and body products,

00:13:14.432 --> 00:13:20.000
around baby products, and
around skin and beauty products.

00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:28.008
For me, I had to distill my idea down
to the thing that was trendy and

00:13:28.008 --> 00:13:33.585
hot in the moment where
investors were excited

00:13:33.585 --> 00:13:38.734
to invest into these new business models.

00:13:38.734 --> 00:13:44.392
And I knew that I didn't want to take
this concept to a retailer because I

00:13:44.392 --> 00:13:49.862
didn't want the retailer to then,
because when you take your concept and

00:13:49.862 --> 00:13:54.790
your idea and you build inside
of a retailer's environment.

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:57.861
They essentially get
to take the reigns and

00:13:57.861 --> 00:14:01.880
mold it into what fits
into their business model.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:05.030
And you often will
compromise on your values,

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:08.460
especially when you
are right out the gate.

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:12.930
I also knew I wanted there to be a charity
component, and I thought that was just

00:14:12.930 --> 00:14:18.010
really important and I also really wanted
there to be diversity and inclusion.

00:14:18.010 --> 00:14:21.590
Just embedded in the values and
the practices of who we are.

00:14:21.590 --> 00:14:28.104
So all of those things I would
say are just very new concepts,

00:14:28.104 --> 00:14:33.213
in apply in,
especially back in ten years ago,

00:14:33.213 --> 00:14:37.815
people hadn't really seen successful bus

00:14:37.815 --> 00:14:42.447
businesses that have values, like that.

00:14:42.447 --> 00:14:45.842
I mean there's like
there were very few and

00:14:45.842 --> 00:14:49.341
far between that hit on
all of those things.

00:14:49.341 --> 00:14:55.180
And so I had to figure out like know
your audience distill the concept

00:14:55.180 --> 00:15:01.540
down to what is hot in the moment that
people are excited to invest behind.

00:15:01.540 --> 00:15:06.713
And make it, get your elevator
pitch down because I have

00:15:06.713 --> 00:15:12.107
like 100 page deck with
everything from home paints and

00:15:12.107 --> 00:15:14.988
furnishings and detergents.

00:15:14.988 --> 00:15:18.805
All the way through to beauty products and

00:15:18.805 --> 00:15:25.120
then brands that sit in different
price points and a distribution.

00:15:25.120 --> 00:15:26.251
&gt;&gt; Like a [INAUDIBLE] site.

00:15:26.251 --> 00:15:28.750
&gt;&gt; I mean,
I had the whole kit and caboodle.

00:15:28.750 --> 00:15:32.063
And so I had to really distill it down to,
at the time,

00:15:32.063 --> 00:15:36.650
what was hot was a predictable
subscription business model.

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:44.005
And I partnered with essentially
the guy that had paved the way for

00:15:44.005 --> 00:15:48.160
that business model to come to life.

00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:53.720
He founded LegalZoom and
another business called ShoeDazzle.

00:15:53.720 --> 00:16:01.263
And so I partnered with him and pitched
him the business and he turned me down.

00:16:01.263 --> 00:16:05.766
And then he came back about 18 months
later and I pitched him again.

00:16:05.766 --> 00:16:10.744
And at that point he had had a kid and
saw his wife change her beauty habits,

00:16:10.744 --> 00:16:13.920
change what she allowed in the house.

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:18.119
Throughout all of their
detergents in their home,

00:16:18.119 --> 00:16:23.883
the cleaning products and basically
was shopping from a very small and

00:16:23.883 --> 00:16:30.540
curated set to provide a healthy
environment for their their little child.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:36.321
And so he was like, okay, so
people do change when they had kids and

00:16:36.321 --> 00:16:40.864
they start thinking
differently about gradients,

00:16:40.864 --> 00:16:46.147
chemical exposures, the planet and
those types of values.

00:16:46.147 --> 00:16:52.037
I would say now, and so
when I partnered with him,

00:16:52.037 --> 00:16:57.086
then we basically went on pitched sandhill

00:16:57.086 --> 00:17:01.305
like five or six companies and VCs.

00:17:01.305 --> 00:17:05.375
And we got five term sheets by the end of
the day and I was eight and a half months

00:17:05.375 --> 00:17:09.200
pregnant and I was told by my doctor,
I wasn't allowed to fly anymore.

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:11.291
And that was my second child.

00:17:11.291 --> 00:17:17.249
I was pregnant because it took me actually
three years to get to that point and

00:17:17.249 --> 00:17:23.220
yeah I think you have to be very humble
if you want to be an entrepreneur.

00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:27.844
Or be part of a business,
know what you know what we know

00:17:27.844 --> 00:17:32.774
what you don't know and
the surround yourself with people

00:17:32.774 --> 00:17:37.724
that really support your strengths and
your weaknesses.

00:17:37.724 --> 00:17:42.439
&gt;&gt; Yeah I love that point about
knowing your audience and how his life

00:17:42.439 --> 00:17:48.627
circumstances changed how he acted as an
audience member to receiving your pitch/.

00:17:48.627 --> 00:17:53.812
And just that little moment of his
wife experiencing exactly what you're

00:17:53.812 --> 00:17:59.179
talking about was all they really needed
to see as a benefit for the company.

00:17:59.179 --> 00:18:03.870
So at business school in all of our
classes, we're constantly reading

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:08.540
case studies to learn about pivotal
moments in a company's history.

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:11.130
So we talked a little bit about the pitch,

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:14.760
we can fast forward to honest
CO is now an amazing company.

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:19.544
What do you think would be the inflection
point that you would choose if the GSB

00:18:19.544 --> 00:18:21.024
were to do a case study?

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:31.571
&gt;&gt; It's interesting,
I would say going from and

00:18:31.571 --> 00:18:35.473
operating from a startup and

00:18:35.473 --> 00:18:40.513
that's a high-growth startup with

00:18:40.513 --> 00:18:46.543
that sort of high-growth business model.

00:18:46.543 --> 00:18:52.056
And really transitioning
into a more steady

00:18:52.056 --> 00:18:56.824
state growth business model [COUGH] and

00:18:56.824 --> 00:19:03.380
building the foundational
principles to operate,

00:19:03.380 --> 00:19:10.840
like a business that could be around for
100 years.

00:19:10.840 --> 00:19:15.963
Because the people that you bring in and
that can get capital

00:19:15.963 --> 00:19:20.985
to start a company are very
different people that usually I

00:19:20.985 --> 00:19:26.331
would say 99% of the time is
usually a very different team.

00:19:26.331 --> 00:19:30.853
That can operate and
build out foundational principles for

00:19:30.853 --> 00:19:35.020
a company that can stick around for
100 plus years.

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:39.143
&gt;&gt; I love that, and
speaking of teams actually,

00:19:39.143 --> 00:19:42.692
an instrumental part of your
team is your current CEO.

00:19:42.692 --> 00:19:46.871
I'm curious, how did you find him?

00:19:46.871 --> 00:19:50.366
How did he convince you that
he was the right person?

00:19:50.366 --> 00:19:53.633
That he was really going to be able
to carry your vision forward as CEO.

00:19:53.633 --> 00:19:56.835
&gt;&gt; Nick worked at Clorox and actually,

00:19:56.835 --> 00:20:02.516
when we were starting the business
when we had such high growth and

00:20:02.516 --> 00:20:07.707
being a digital first business,
and building community.

00:20:07.707 --> 00:20:11.392
It was such a different approach and
the values, and the ethics,

00:20:11.392 --> 00:20:13.201
around how the wellness again,

00:20:13.201 --> 00:20:16.426
was also such a different in
the way that we went about it.

00:20:16.426 --> 00:20:22.505
Was such a different approach to the way
a lot of the big CPG companies operate.

00:20:22.505 --> 00:20:26.301
And so we've had all of the big
companies knock on our door and

00:20:26.301 --> 00:20:28.645
you have to take those meetings.

00:20:28.645 --> 00:20:34.369
And so he had spent time with us and

00:20:34.369 --> 00:20:39.276
looked at us years before and

00:20:39.276 --> 00:20:47.461
really sort of understood
our business model.

00:20:47.461 --> 00:20:52.290
And then when we were at time to bring
in a new team to really build those

00:20:52.290 --> 00:20:58.012
foundational principles
&gt;&gt; I think because he had

00:20:58.012 --> 00:21:05.255
historic data and understood the mental
model of like what needed to be true.

00:21:05.255 --> 00:21:12.462
And also he had done it with bird bees
when they brought bird bees into Clorox.

00:21:12.462 --> 00:21:20.466
And, he really has a very
creative approach to business and

00:21:20.466 --> 00:21:25.909
I think you can't have someone who's so

00:21:25.909 --> 00:21:29.601
