the business needs at that moment.

00:21:39.102 --> 00:21:43.971
And he really felt like he
was grounded and in and

00:21:43.971 --> 00:21:49.715
having conviction about that but
then also I would say.

00:21:49.715 --> 00:21:54.509
The other big piece of it is he really
lived this life these values are very

00:21:54.509 --> 00:21:55.853
meaningful to him.

00:21:55.853 --> 00:22:00.322
And his family he's a father
of four two sets of twins and

00:22:00.322 --> 00:22:05.359
his wife and him and the kids they
live very much these values and

00:22:05.359 --> 00:22:10.220
these are the types of products
they have in their home.

00:22:10.220 --> 00:22:16.225
And this is how they live their life so
just those the purpose and

00:22:16.225 --> 00:22:21.681
the mission of the company
really resonated with him so.

00:22:21.681 --> 00:22:22.876
&gt;&gt; Have that, well,

00:22:22.876 --> 00:22:27.743
an amazing moment that you had last year
was when you took the company public.

00:22:27.743 --> 00:22:29.837
So congratulations not.

00:22:29.837 --> 00:22:31.228
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm curious.

00:22:31.228 --> 00:22:34.220
Did the IPO feel like
a moment to pause and

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:37.870
appreciate all of your accomplishments?

00:22:37.870 --> 00:22:41.185
Or was it a wake up call
that new challenges and

00:22:41.185 --> 00:22:43.725
opportunities were ahead of you?

00:22:43.725 --> 00:22:46.950
For me, it was both.

00:22:46.950 --> 00:22:53.827
I would say that every time we hit
a different sort of transition or

00:22:53.827 --> 00:23:01.222
milestone, at this point I feel like
I've been through [LAUGH] it all.

00:23:01.222 --> 00:23:03.880
And you'll see guys when you start
your own businesses [LAUGH].

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:11.911
And yeah, it almost felt like
we're starting from zero again and

00:23:11.911 --> 00:23:19.270
building the foundation of what it means
to be and operate like a public company.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:23.585
And now like we're building
those building blocks and

00:23:23.585 --> 00:23:28.568
those foundational principles again,
but in this new space.

00:23:28.568 --> 00:23:33.998
&gt;&gt; I love that, so I would be remiss if
I didn't mention your previous role and

00:23:33.998 --> 00:23:38.825
current still role in Hollywood and
where I learned at least at your

00:23:38.825 --> 00:23:43.970
talent agency CAA branding your
personal brand is everything.

00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:47.794
And I'm curious what learnings
carried over from that world.

00:23:47.794 --> 00:23:51.921
&gt;&gt; To the business world
I'm now at UTA but

00:23:51.921 --> 00:23:57.250
I yeah I would say that
your personal brand

00:23:57.250 --> 00:24:02.914
As someone in Hollywood but
then even someone like you or

00:24:02.914 --> 00:24:07.380
any of you guys sitting there, you can't.

00:24:09.310 --> 00:24:11.673
You guys are living in a very kind of,

00:24:11.673 --> 00:24:15.760
it's sort of like welcome to
the party of being exposed.

00:24:15.760 --> 00:24:21.790
You guys are living
a very digital reality.

00:24:21.790 --> 00:24:27.070
And people pan on Earth who you are and
your past and

00:24:27.070 --> 00:24:32.476
your life, if anything,
is much more on display,

00:24:32.476 --> 00:24:37.003
I would say, than any other generation and

00:24:37.003 --> 00:24:40.435
will continue to be on display.

00:24:40.435 --> 00:24:45.349
And so where you go in your life and
who you surround

00:24:45.349 --> 00:24:50.614
yourself with and
how you align yourself along the way

00:24:50.614 --> 00:24:55.653
will have an impact on
your future opportunities.

00:24:55.653 --> 00:25:03.340
And so I think for me, I was always very
cognizant of that from a very early age.

00:25:03.340 --> 00:25:08.465
And I think it's just something to
be mindful of depending on what

00:25:08.465 --> 00:25:14.073
your aspirations are, making sure
that you own all of your decisions.

00:25:14.073 --> 00:25:19.720
And you are honest with
yourself along the way and

00:25:19.720 --> 00:25:23.991
I'm not saying don't be fearful but

00:25:23.991 --> 00:25:29.224
just be aware and
I think a lot of people don't

00:25:29.224 --> 00:25:34.340
always think of step three, four and five.

00:25:35.490 --> 00:25:41.210
They just see the shiny penny in
front of them, and they get excited.

00:25:41.210 --> 00:25:45.560
But I always think of three, four and
five because I didn't have a backup plan.

00:25:45.560 --> 00:25:48.190
I didn't grow up with a safety net.

00:25:48.190 --> 00:25:49.340
I had to create my own.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:55.332
So I think I just operated with that
in mind when I was building out or

00:25:55.332 --> 00:25:58.760
making any decision from age 12 on.

00:25:59.770 --> 00:26:04.279
&gt;&gt; Right, yeah and important to note that
it started so young and social media and

00:26:04.279 --> 00:26:08.789
everything has changed so much and
you've been adapting since you were 12,

00:26:08.789 --> 00:26:10.113
which was is amazing.

00:26:11.510 --> 00:26:15.260
I'd love to talk about the future
of where honest is going.

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:17.674
You recently launched the daily defense

00:26:17.674 --> 00:26:19.040
collection,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:26:19.040 --> 00:26:20.463
&gt;&gt; Which is a line of skincare

00:26:20.463 --> 00:26:24.541
products designed to defend against
environmental pollutants which is so

00:26:24.541 --> 00:26:26.340
incredibly important.

00:26:26.340 --> 00:26:28.650
I'm curious how do you-
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] screens,

00:26:28.650 --> 00:26:32.644
who knew these screens could
affect your skin, my god.

00:26:32.644 --> 00:26:37.219
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] The screens that are currently
blasting stuff at us right now.

00:26:37.219 --> 00:26:42.520
&gt;&gt; All day [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm curious how you predict

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:48.144
future trends and
continue to build products for the future.

00:26:48.144 --> 00:26:56.133
&gt;&gt; A lot of it comes from I think
trusting my gut and my intuition.

00:26:56.133 --> 00:27:02.350
It's weird like I don't
necessarily need McKinsey data,

00:27:02.350 --> 00:27:08.920
to substantiate where I like,
what I feel or what I know.

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:15.548
And oftentimes, I feel like I can be
in not everything is a home run but

00:27:15.548 --> 00:27:20.919
if you will need a bain or
McKinsey study to validate your

00:27:20.919 --> 00:27:26.658
marketplace opportunity,
it's often a little too late.

00:27:26.658 --> 00:27:32.283
Because the trendsetters
did it three years before,

00:27:32.283 --> 00:27:39.785
it became a trend that it would even
become a case study or a study at all.

00:27:39.785 --> 00:27:44.707
And so I would say that keeping
your feet on the ground and

00:27:44.707 --> 00:27:49.737
really understanding the pain point or
the needs that you

00:27:49.737 --> 00:27:54.478
are filling in whatever
business you're creating.

00:27:54.478 --> 00:28:02.250
For me it's being very consumer
centric and very personal.

00:28:02.250 --> 00:28:07.921
I always start from that place and
then I do my research from there

00:28:07.921 --> 00:28:14.222
on around sort of like what other
companies are doing things like this or

00:28:14.222 --> 00:28:18.339
what other like our
sensitive skin care line.

00:28:18.339 --> 00:28:23.695
All of us want benefits in our
skin care we want brightening and

00:28:23.695 --> 00:28:26.682
we want our fine lines to go away and

00:28:26.682 --> 00:28:30.709
we want you know our
discoloration to go away.

00:28:30.709 --> 00:28:37.457
We want to have those skincare benefits
in our skincare we just don't.

00:28:37.457 --> 00:28:43.034
We're not looking for something that's
just sort of like, put this on your face

00:28:43.034 --> 00:28:48.710
and it'll be a shield and then there you
go and it's better than having this rush.

00:28:48.710 --> 00:28:51.411
Now, I'm allergic to
a lot of those products,

00:28:51.411 --> 00:28:54.890
even the gentle ones that
the dermatologists recommend.

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:56.690
So I couldn't even use those.

00:28:56.690 --> 00:29:01.588
So I was like, how about we have
a skin care routine that works for

00:29:01.588 --> 00:29:07.506
people with really sensitive skin but
actually like does something for you.

00:29:07.506 --> 00:29:12.513
And our head chemists she has
very sensitive skin as well.

00:29:12.513 --> 00:29:18.849
And so that's just something where we were
like hey if you get rashes if you have

00:29:18.849 --> 00:29:25.295
sensitive skin a lot of people who break
out a lot also have very sensitive skin.

00:29:25.295 --> 00:29:29.004
There's nothing for you and
you're sort of just left in the dust.

00:29:29.004 --> 00:29:34.610
So I was like this would be a cool one
to be able to create a full solution

00:29:34.610 --> 00:29:40.233
where you can have exfoliation,
brightening, really hydrating.

00:29:40.233 --> 00:29:44.848
We have a lightweight moisturizer and
a heavier moisturizer so it's for

00:29:44.848 --> 00:29:47.020
different types of skin.

00:29:47.020 --> 00:29:50.030
It doesn't clog your pores,
all of those things.

00:29:50.030 --> 00:29:53.680
So that's another a lot of it
comes from the personal and

00:29:53.680 --> 00:29:58.429
then you look at the marketplace and
you see how many people are doing it.

00:29:58.429 --> 00:30:02.519
And then there's other things where you're
just like hey, this is table stakes.

00:30:02.519 --> 00:30:07.049
You need to have X or
you need to have Y in order

00:30:07.049 --> 00:30:11.650
to really like be in this category.

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:13.448
So, it's a blend.

00:30:13.448 --> 00:30:16.004
&gt;&gt; Yeah,
I love how your ideation process for

00:30:16.004 --> 00:30:20.193
new products kind of always goes back
to how you started honest itself,

00:30:20.193 --> 00:30:23.825
which was based on problems
that you experienced yourself.

00:30:23.825 --> 00:30:27.490
And obviously you're not
the only one to experience those.

00:30:27.490 --> 00:30:30.643
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; So we started this talk with a TikTok.

00:30:30.643 --> 00:30:37.370
[LAUGH] You are a parent to Gen Z kids and
a marketer.

00:30:37.370 --> 00:30:41.577
And I'm curious because we
are always talking about Gen Z and

00:30:41.577 --> 00:30:47.117
I feel like articles are always Gen Z this
Gen Z that as a parent and as a marketer.

00:30:47.117 --> 00:30:50.711
What is something you think we're
getting wrong about that generation?

00:30:50.711 --> 00:30:54.959
&gt;&gt; I think we try to overcomplicate it and

00:30:54.959 --> 00:30:59.487
the gap of understanding feels so wide,

00:31:01.490 --> 00:31:06.215
And I think there's sort
of like a frustration in

00:31:06.215 --> 00:31:10.134
the way that they're moving at a pace and

00:31:10.134 --> 00:31:15.204
are going to continue to move
at a pace that is beyond any

00:31:15.204 --> 00:31:20.180
other generation just
because in the digital age.

00:31:20.180 --> 00:31:24.635
Before you had to reimagine your
business sort of like every five years.

00:31:24.635 --> 00:31:28.967
And then I would say about ten years ago
when I launched honest, it was like no,

00:31:28.967 --> 00:31:32.285
you've gotta reimagine your business and
how you operate and

00:31:32.285 --> 00:31:34.480
the way that you do it every three years.

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:38.110
Now, I would say it's like every
year you've gotta reassess and

00:31:38.110 --> 00:31:39.696
reevaluate and reimagine.

00:31:42.397 --> 00:31:46.534
I think that because they're so

00:31:46.534 --> 00:31:50.370
young, maybe it's hard to,

00:31:52.892 --> 00:31:58.850
Trust that they know what they're
talking about and allow them to.

00:31:59.980 --> 00:32:07.097
Take some leadership and hand the baton
over like know what you don't know and

00:32:07.097 --> 00:32:11.916
also like,
if something is meant to appeal to them it

00:32:11.916 --> 00:32:17.970
doesn't necessarily need to
appeal to me or to you, right?

00:32:17.970 --> 00:32:21.890
And sometimes it'll appeal to me and
it'll appeal to you.

00:32:21.890 --> 00:32:27.482
But I think that is one thing that like,
I feel like when you're running

00:32:27.482 --> 00:32:32.516
a business or when you're trying
to understand a demographic,

00:32:32.516 --> 00:32:36.987
you feel like you should also
be on the same page as them but

00:32:36.987 --> 00:32:41.670
it's totally okay that they
think the way that they think.

00:32:41.670 --> 00:32:45.400
And they respond to things differently
than you and you don't need to take it

00:32:45.400 --> 00:32:49.280
personally and know that it's okay for
you to have your differences.

00:32:49.280 --> 00:32:53.972
What I appreciate about Gen Z and
I actually don't think of it

00:32:53.972 --> 00:32:58.650
as being an issue is just
that they love authenticity.

00:32:58.650 --> 00:33:00.190
They are values driven.

00:33:02.540 --> 00:33:06.134
And the brand values
are meaningful to them and

00:33:06.134 --> 00:33:09.914
the more that you really
show up authentically,

00:33:09.914 --> 00:33:14.246
the better I think the businesses
that are in trouble and

00:33:14.246 --> 00:33:18.880
appealing to Gen Z are the ones
that are trying to be tricky.

00:33:21.010 --> 00:33:24.685
&gt;&gt; Well a clear of today for
sure is knowing your audience and

00:33:24.685 --> 00:33:27.300
you clearly know this audience as well.

00:33:28.610 --> 00:33:32.710
So much about honest Co is
educating the consumer.

00:33:32.710 --> 00:33:36.010
You've been doing that since
before you even started.

00:33:36.010 --> 00:33:40.530
The company as many of us here
are looking to start new businesses.

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:45.850
Some of them might be quite complicated
deep tech, web three, what have you.

00:33:45.850 --> 00:33:51.560
What lessons can you share about what
works best in educating your consumer?

00:33:53.489 --> 00:33:58.441
&gt;&gt; Make it entertaining if it's boring for
you to watch,

00:33:58.441 --> 00:34:02.710
it's going to be boring for
anybody to watch.

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:06.100
What I mean, if it's boring to read it,
it's going to be boring for everyone.

00:34:07.930 --> 00:34:11.760
I think more and more because we have so

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:17.145
much It's almost like
the volume of content, it is so

00:34:17.145 --> 00:34:22.173
daunting and overwhelming and
is only going to get

00:34:22.173 --> 00:34:28.296
more daunting as we start to
integrate AR into our day to day.

00:34:28.296 --> 00:34:33.264
And as we integrate more
of the Web 3 into our

00:34:33.264 --> 00:34:37.696
day to day with wearables and all that,

00:34:37.696 --> 00:34:43.474
we're going to almost have
information overload and

00:34:43.474 --> 00:34:47.369
so having really short and sweet,

00:34:47.369 --> 00:34:51.509
pithy Ways of communicating,

00:34:51.509 --> 00:34:57.710
no matter if you're a software company or
not even a B2B.

00:34:59.140 --> 00:35:02.684
I would say when we're
choosing partners for

00:35:02.684 --> 00:35:08.000
the B2B type of partners are something
that will help a function for

00:35:08.000 --> 00:35:11.372
in our company and
in some form or fashion.

00:35:11.372 --> 00:35:16.120
We're always looking at how
difficult is it to integrate and

00:35:16.120 --> 00:35:20.860
usually the difficulty has
to do with how boring it is.

00:35:20.860 --> 00:35:25.120
To [LAUGH] understand it and to apply it.

00:35:25.120 --> 00:35:29.522
In making your materials as consumer

00:35:29.522 --> 00:35:34.350
facing as possible is important even for

00:35:34.350 --> 00:35:38.187
software companies or B2B is.

00:35:38.187 --> 00:35:40.630
&gt;&gt; That's great advice.

00:35:40.630 --> 00:35:41.140
Thank you.

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:44.790
So you've shared incredible
advice with all of us.

00:35:44.790 --> 00:35:47.240
And we're all super grateful for
your time.

00:35:47.240 --> 00:35:52.118
And you're also an incredible model for
so many founders from underrepresented

00:35:52.118 --> 00:35:55.950
groups and for women, so
thank you for being here today.

00:35:55.950 --> 00:36:00.140
I could go on forever, but we do have
some questions from the audience.

00:36:00.140 --> 00:36:04.080
So I would like to to turn it over
to our first question from Jay Why.

00:36:06.470 --> 00:36:08.120
&gt;&gt; Hi, Jessica.

00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:12.180
I'm originally from Singapore and
moved to the Bay to attend a GSB.

00:36:12.180 --> 00:36:16.310
I'm an aspiring entrepreneur,
so super excited to be here.

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:20.774
And my question for you is, what are some
of your biggest fears in building

00:36:20.774 --> 00:36:23.870
the honest core and
how do you overcome them?

00:36:23.870 --> 00:36:31.578
&gt;&gt; Maybe my biggest fear was not
connecting with my truth in a way and

00:36:31.578 --> 00:36:35.576
allowing people who had trust and

00:36:35.576 --> 00:36:42.143
a history in business sort of
take over the narrative or

00:36:42.143 --> 00:36:51.508
run the business in a direction that
I intuitively knew wasn't right.

00:36:51.508 --> 00:36:56.787
&gt;&gt; And
I felt imposter syndrome because I didn't

00:36:56.787 --> 00:37:03.610
have a traditional business degree and
backgrounds.

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:10.714
And so it took me a hot minute to really
like step into my skin as a person and

00:37:10.714 --> 00:37:16.185
I think the more I've trusted
my intuition and my gut and

00:37:16.185 --> 00:37:22.134
stood in my truth,
the better I feel in everything, right?

00:37:22.134 --> 00:37:24.780
In business and and in life.

00:37:24.780 --> 00:37:28.336
But I would say that was my biggest fear.

00:37:30.140 --> 00:37:32.987
It's scary when you've never
done it before, right?

00:37:32.987 --> 00:37:36.950
Absolutely thanks for sharing.

00:37:36.950 --> 00:37:37.680
&gt;&gt; Hi Jessica.

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:41.662
My name is Kristen Lim and
I am also an aspiring entrepreneur,

00:37:41.662 --> 00:37:45.820
looking to do something
within the beauty space.

00:37:45.820 --> 00:37:51.209
I'm also a power user of honest products
and I can't live without my magic mom.

